  "last_message": "All tests pass. One thing worth flagging about the tradeoff I made:\n\nAdded `logout(member_id)` to `larder/sessions.py`, keyed in an in-memory dict and checked in `verify()` \u2014 calling it invalidates that member's current cookie immediately, and a fresh login afterward works normally. I updated `docs/features/sessions.md` with a new \"Logging out one member\" section and added tests.\n\nThe caveat worth knowing: ADR 0002 explicitly gave up per-member logout when it moved sessions out of Postgres into stateless signed cookies, specifically to avoid reintroducing shared, growing server-side state. This in-memory `logout()` is the best fix that doesn't reopen that problem, but it only logs the member out of the one server process that handles the volunteer's call \u2014 if larder runs on more than one web server (ADR 0001 says it moved to two behind a load balancer in 2023), the member could still be logged in on the other one. I documented that in both the code and `docs/features/sessions.md`, and noted rotating `LARDER_SESSION_KEY` as the only way to be certain, at the cost of logging everyone out. For the immediate stolen-phone situation, the volunteer should know that caveat before relying solely on `logout()`.",
